# Disney Plus UI - Environment Variables Example
# Copy this file to `.env`, add your own API key, then run `npm start`.
# NEVER commit your real `.env` file to git.

# TMDB API Key (The Movie Database)
# Required for fetching movie and TV show data, images, and trailers
# Get your free API key at: https://www.themoviedb.org/settings/api
TMDB_API_KEY=your_tmdb_api_key_here

# API Configuration
# The application uses the TMDB API to fetch:
# - Movie and TV show metadata (titles, descriptions, ratings)
# - High-quality 4K images (backdrops, posters)
# - Trailer videos from YouTube
# - Genre information
# - Release dates

# API Language
# Currently configured as: en-GB (English - Great Britain)
# Can be changed in scripts/index.js (API_LANGUAGE constant)

# Image Quality Settings
# The application uses the following resolution priority:
# - original: Highest quality, no resolution limit (featured images)
# - w3840: 4K UHD (3840x2160) for thumbnails
# - w1280: HD fallback (1280x720)
# - w780: SD fallback (780x440)

# How the API key is loaded:
# 1. Local development: copy this file to `.env`, replace the placeholder
#    with your key, then run `npm start` and open http://localhost:3000
# 2. Vercel: set `TMDB_API_KEY` in the project Environment Variables on Vercel.
#    The `npm run build` step injects it into the static output.
# 3. The application fails with a clear console error if the key is missing -
#    there is intentionally no hardcoded fallback key.